          {
            "typeName": "dsDescription",
            "multiple": true,
            "typeClass": "compound",
            "value": [
              {
                "dsDescriptionValue": {
                  "typeName": "dsDescriptionValue",
                  "multiple": false,
                  "typeClass": "primitive",
                  "value": "Methods: An exploratory qualitative study was conducted, using in-depth interviews with 28 people living with prolonged incurable cancer for more than one year. A narrative approach was used to explore participants’ lived experiences, allowing space for meaning-making. Data were thematically analysed. \nResults: Three main themes emerged from the interviews: 1) the daily experience of living with prolonged incurable cancer, marked by ongoing uncertainty and evolving challenges; 2) social and personal impact; and 3) coping strategies and meaning-making. While attempting to (re)integrate into their everyday environments and routines, participants reported unmet needs for psychosocial support and struggled with a lack of understanding from others. Navigating these circumstances required considerable energy, which was often limited. \nConclusion: People living with prolonged incurable cancer experience enormous disruptions to their well-being and face unique psychosocial challenges. They seek recognition and understanding and express a need for healthcare professionals to better address their ongoing support needs in facing a future filled with uncertainty."
                }
              }
            ]
          },
